PART 2: WRITING
──────────────────────────────

W1 — Section 3 (Design)
Addresses: design justification hard to see, prose rough (A's design questions, D's "what is RAN-specific").
How: Rephrase only, no structural content change — add more formulas and follow ML-paper writing conventions (symbolic definitions of inputs/outputs, module transformations, loss functions), making the design description more precise and rigorous. The justifications reviewers question (e.g., why only masked KPI reconstruction) already exist in the Discussion.

W2 — Novelty Presentation
Addresses: read as "an assembly of existing techniques" (B, C, D). The content is already in the paper; this is a visibility issue.
How: Strengthen in the Introduction and the Discussion — reword the Intro contribution list from "what we did" to "what RAN-specific problem it solves," explicitly positioning the contribution at the systems/domain level; consolidate and sharpen the novelty argument in the Discussion.

W3 — LLM-Branch Contradiction + Core/Pro Boundary
Addresses: the apparent contradiction between the LLM branch and "LLMs perform poorly on regression" (A, B, D); Pro's operating scenario and cost/benefit (D, also covering C's compute concern).
How: Clarify the contradiction in the Discussion — "poor LLM-only numerical regression ≠ useless LLM features as an auxiliary branch"; the LLM branch provides semantic representation enhancement while numerical prediction remains with the dedicated head. Emphasize the Core/Pro usage boundary in the experiment section — using the 168x memory figures to state when Core suffices and when Pro is worth it.

W4 — RAN Operational Background
Addresses: B's (and similarly D's) "unclear problem formulation" — the paper lacks operational grounding for RAN management: what task types exist day-to-day, how decision-making works, and which data modalities are involved, so readers cannot judge whether the model design targets real operational needs (what is asked for is operational background, not mathematical formalization).
How: No new section — expand the existing background in Sections 2 and 4. In Section 2, add a structured overview of typical RAN workflows, decision processes, and data modalities, and front-load here the motivation for why a pure LLM or a generic time-series FM would not suffice; in Section 4, add the corresponding operational context at each use case, anchoring the task definitions in real decision workflows.

W5 — Absolute-Performance Discussion
Addresses: low absolute AUPRC 0.286 / F1 0.318, missing performance-ceiling discussion (A).
How: Reinforce at Use Case 1 (RANPerf) in Section 4 — state the inherently very low positive-class ratio, the rationale for the 30% threshold, missing information (neighboring-cell interference, mobility, spectrum) bounding the ceiling, and imbalance handling during training; establish that the low absolute numbers reflect task difficulty, not model weakness.

W6 — Field Deployment Section
Addresses: badly under-specified field study (E's central question), missing statistical treatment (A), EngSav reporting only MAD (A).
How: Rewrite the field deployment section — clearly state the nature of the deployment (whether production configurations were actually changed with measured outcomes, region, number of variables changed, duration); for EngSav, explain why MAD is used and the availability of actual energy-savings data; fold in the CI/significance results if E3.1 is run.

